  "unit": "Possession, nested within lineup (stint) and team-game.",
  "sample": "NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25 regular season and playoffs. The 2025-26 season will be excluded or used only as a hold-out test set due to partial data. Only non-garbage time possessions will be included. Players will be assigned structural roles (Primary Organizer, Shaper, Terminal Scorer, Role Occupant/Specialist) based on their performance over a full season (or a significant portion like a half-season) to ensure stability and sufficient data for influence calculation. These player roles will then be mapped to lineups.",
  "power": "Player-level structural roles will be derived from a minimum threshold of initiations (e.g., 500 initiations per player per season) to ensure reliable calculation of structural influence. Lineup-level analyses will require a minimum number of possessions per lineup (e.g., 50 possessions) to estimate offensive efficiency reliably. With 420,993 possessions and 90 team-seasons, there is sufficient data to classify players and analyze common lineups, but specific rare lineup configurations may still lack power. A power analysis will be conducted for the primary estimand to determine minimum detectable effect sizes given the observed distribution of lineup types.",
  "controls": "Individual player-level controls: Usage rate, individual PPP, true shooting percentage, individual offensive rating, minutes played. Team-level controls: Opponent strength (e.g., defensive rating), game context (home/away, score differential, period), coaching staff, season, team-level talent (e.g., average talent of players on the court as measured by a composite metric).",
  "falsification": "1. Test if randomly assigned 'structural roles' (while maintaining the overall distribution of roles) show similar explanatory power. 2. Test if roles derived from purely talent-based metrics (e.g., high usage, high efficiency) without structural influence components can explain as much variation. 3. Test for 'placebo' effects by examining defensive efficiency outcomes, which should not be directly impacted by offensive structural roles in the same way.",
  "multiplicity": "If multiple specific hypotheses about different role combinations or individual role effects are tested, a multiple testing correction (e.g., Bonferroni, FDR) will be applied. The primary estimand will focus on a pre-specified aggregate measure of 'structural coverage' or a single, most impactful role combination.",
  "decision_rule": "The primary hypothesis is that lineup structural coverage, as defined by the presence of specific role categories, explains a statistically significant (p < 0.05) and substantively meaningful portion of the variance in offensive efficiency, beyond what can be explained by player talent and usage. The 'behavior' of talent control will be assessed by examining the coefficients and significance of talent metrics in the model; they should align with established basketball principles (e.g., more talented players correlating with higher efficiency).",
  "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 regular season and playoffs, NBA 2023-24 regular season and playoffs, NBA 2024-25 regular season and playoffs. Play-by-play, rotation, matchups, summary, and starters files from the NBA stats API, alongside derived play-type possession records."
 }
}

